.TH P910ND 8 "16 January 2014"
.SH NAME
p910nd \- port 9100+n printer daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p910nd
[\fB-f device\fR]
[\fB-i bindaddr\fR]
[\fB-t timeout\fR]
[\fB-u user\fR]
[\fB-g group\fR]
[\fB-bvd\fR]
[\fB0|...|9\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I p910nd
is a small daemon that copies any data received on the port
it is listening on to the corresponding printer port.
It is primarily intended for diskless Linux hosts running as printer drivers
but there is no reason why it could not be used on diskful hosts.
Ports 9100 through 9109 are copied to /dev/lp0 through /dev/lp9.
The default is port 9100 to /dev/lp0.
.LP
The \fB-f\fR option can be used to specify a different printer device,
e.g. /dev/usblp0.
.LP
If the printer device cannot be opened -- a USB printer that is not plugged in
yet, for instance -- the daemon keeps retrying rather than exiting, and accepts
no connection until the device is available.  A client that connects meanwhile
is queued by the kernel and served as soon as the printer appears, instead of
being accepted and then left waiting indefinitely.
.LP
The \fB-i\fR option can be used to specify binding to one address instead
of all interfaces which is the default.
.LP
The \fB-t\fR option sets the idle timeout in seconds: a job during which neither
the network nor the printer has produced anything for that long is treated as
finished and the connection is closed.  A value of 0 disables the idle timer
altogether.  The default is 5 seconds.  In unidirectional mode the timer is armed
only when \fB-t\fR is given explicitly, because those jobs were never timed out
before and a slow client or a slow printer may pause one for minutes; the job is
never closed while data is still buffered, so no byte can be lost this way.
.LP
The same value bounds the short grace period that is kept after the host has
finished sending, which is what allows a printer reply arriving just after that
point to be forwarded.  With \fB-t 0\fR that grace period reverts to the
compile-time default of 5 seconds, so a job always terminates.
.LP
The \fB-u\fR and \fB-g\fR options make the daemon give up privileges and continue
as the named user and group, which may also be given numerically.  Both are
optional and default to no change, so existing setups are unaffected.  The switch
happens after the privileged work is finished (lock file, pid file and the
listening socket), but before any printer device is opened; since the device is
re-opened for every job, the target account must be able to open it, or jobs will
not start.  Running unprivileged is the safer configuration wherever the device
permissions allow it.
.LP
The \fB-b\fR option turns on bidirectional copying.
.LP
The \fB-v\fR option shows the version number.
.LP
The \fB-d\fR option causes the daemon to run in the foreground in
standalone mode and prints log messages to stdout for debugging.
.SH INSTALLATION
.I p910nd
can be run as a standalone daemon or from (x)inetd.
It will automatically detect if it is running under (x)inetd.
.LP
A sample SysVinit script,
.IR p910nd.sh ,
is provided for operation as a daemon.
.I p910nd
will change its name under ps to match the printer port, i.e.
.I p9100d
through
.IR p9109d .
.LP
When running under (x)inetd, the
.I /etc/inetd.conf
entry should look something like this (with tcpwrappers protection):
.sp
.nf
p9101 stream tcp nowait root /usr/sbin/tcpd /sbin/p910nd
.fi
.sp
Don't forget to add an entry in
.I /etc/services
for the corresponding port.
.LP
If operating with lprng, use the host%port syntax for the
printer device to send jobs to it.
.LP
If operating with CUPS, this is supported as the AppSocket
protocol, also known as the JetDirect (probably TM) protocol.
.LP
If operating with classic Berkeley lpd, a sample client,
.IR client.pl ,
is provided.
This should be installed as the ifilter (if=) in /etc/printcap.
.I banner.pl
should be installed as the ofilter (of=) in /etc/printcap.
It may be necessary to create a dummy spool file for lpd (lp=).
This file will be opened but not written to.
The corresponding C versions are left as an exercise for the reader.
.LP
When running under inetd, more than one instance could be started.
To avoid problems with multiple instances attempting to access the
printer at the same time, make sure that only one client is active
at any one time. This can be done by designating one host as the
spooler and sending all jobs to this host. You will probably
need to set up an intermediate queue anyway to provide print job filtering.
.LP
If built with USE_WRAP (or its alias USE_LIBWRAP), it uses the libwrap
library (tcpwrappers).
Access control can be done with /etc/hosts.allow
and /etc/hosts.deny. The service name is p910nd.
.SH SECURITY
.I p910nd
performs no authentication and no filtering of the data it forwards: any host
that can reach the listening port can print, and in bidirectional mode can read
whatever is returned.  Expose it only to trusted clients, and preferably restrict
it further with tcpwrappers
.RB ( USE_WRAP )
or a host firewall.
.LP
Because
.B \-f
names an arbitrary path and
.B \-b
opens that path read-write, using the two together lets any client that can
reach the port read and append to the named file; known attacks of this kind
include CVE-2018-10123.  Do not combine
.B \-b
and
.B \-f
on a file that must stay private, and note that the daemon traditionally runs
as root without dropping privileges.
.LP
A binary may be built so that the device is restricted to an explicit list:
compile with
.B \-DDEVICE_ALLOWLIST='"/dev/lp%c:/dev/usblp%c"'
(a colon separated set of path patterns in which
.B %c
stands for the printer number).  Any other device -- including one passed with
.B \-f
-- is then refused at startup.  The check is compiled out by default, so the
default build behaves exactly as it always has.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I p910nd
logs error messages to syslog.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
printcap(5), hosts_access(5)
.SH FILES
/var/run/p9100d.pid, /var/lock/subsys/p9100d, /etc/hosts.allow, /etc/hosts.deny
.LP
The directories holding the pid and lock files are created if they do not yet
exist, so a host whose /var/run or /var/lock/subsys is missing does not prevent
the daemon from starting.
